  "query_preview": "..\nWater vapor imagery shows a fairly zonal flow across Southeast\nArizona. This will lead to mild conditions with temperatures 1-3\ndegrees warmer than yesterday. Late afternoon breezes can be \nexpected, especially when the sun is going down for the night. \n\nModels are still on track for our change in weather. The low\npressure system will start to track across the area tomorrow.\nMid-level moisture will rise on Sunday, leading to 10-20 percent\nchances for rain showers and thunderstorms late in the afternoon\nto evening. The recent REFS model and the UoF WRF model are \nshowing development from Nogales to Tucson during early \nafternoon and pushing the storm and shower activity north to \nnortheast due to the southerly flow. Chances will hover in the \n10-20 percent in the late night. \n\nMonday will be the more active day with this weather system. The\ndynamics, surface heating, and moisture will be east to\nnortheast of Tucson. Chances will peak with the best chances\ntowards the White Mountains. But, rainfall amounts will be less\nthan a tenth of an inch and therefore be more dry thunderstorm \nthreat. SPC has highlighted a 10 percent chance for isolated dry\nthunderstorm due to this reason.  \n\nWinds will increase as well on Monday due to the tightening of\nthe pressure gradient. A low pressure center will move into the\nGreat Basin region and strengthen the gradient behind the\nMonday's weather system through at least Wednesday. The \nensemble members have started to trend a slower moving low, \npotentially lingering in the region through the rest of the \nweek. If this trend continues with more agreement within the \nensemble model world, Southeast Arizona will see continue \nbreezes for the remainder of the work week.",
